# License

Proxima is dual-licensed under either of

- Apache License, Version 2.0 — see [`LICENSE-APACHE`](LICENSE-APACHE)
- MIT License — see [`LICENSE-MIT`](LICENSE-MIT)

at your option.

## Why dual licensing

- **Apache-2.0** gives companies an explicit patent grant, which is why many
  security teams require it before they will depend on a tool.
- **MIT** keeps the tool trivially embeddable for individuals, scripts, and
  teaching material.

You only need to comply with one of them.

## Contribution licensing

Unless you explicitly state otherwise, any contribution intentionally submitted
for inclusion in this project by you, as defined in the Apache-2.0 license, shall
be dual-licensed as above, without any additional terms or conditions.

By opening a pull request you confirm you have the right to submit the work under
these terms. There is no separate CLA.

## Third-party assets

Bundled scanner definitions under `resources/passive/` describe well-known HTTP
behaviours and reference public documentation (MDN, OWASP). They are original
text released under the same dual license. No third-party scanner rulesets are
vendored.
